    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for my diesel tech exam and i'm a bit stuck. can u explain how exactly a turbocharger works to increase the manifold pressure? like, what's the actual cycle from the exhaust side to the intake side?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A turbocharger uses the energy from hot exhaust gases to spin a turbine wheel. This turbine is physically linked by a shaft to a compressor wheel, which is housed in the intake side. As the turbine spins rapidly, it forces the compressor to draw in more air than the engine would normally. This compressed, higher-density air is then channeled into the intake manifold, resulting in increased manifold pressure and more power.",
      "source": "target",
